                    <div class="sc_text_acc">
                        <div class="anchor_tag" id="discussion"></div>
                        <div class="sc_text_acc_title">
                            <h2 class="notop">Discussion</h2>
                        </div>

                        <div class="sc_text_acc_container  content-display-0">
                            <p class="jove_content">QA-based metrics (e.g., FEQA/QuestEval/QAFactEval) and NLI-based metrics (e.g., FactCC/SummaC) require auxiliary QA/entailment components and often provide sentence-level judgments. In contrast, EHI is a lightweight, reference-free, entity-level score that (i) directly targets the primary failure mode we observe -- entity hallucination; (ii) is model-agnostic and fast to compute; and (iii) serves as a dense reward for RL fine-tuning. Empirically, EHI complements QA/NLI metrics by pinpointing which entities drive factuality errors, enabling targeted improvements without adding heavy QA or entailment pipelines.</p>
<p class="jove_content">The reinforcement-learning framework directly optimizes for entity faithfulness by using the Entity Hallucination Index (EHI) as a dense reward signal<sup class="xref">18</sup>. The metric quantifies the presence, correctness, and grounding of named entities in generated summaries and categorizes hallucinations by type and severity. During fine-tuning, baseline summaries are scored using EHI, and the model is reinforced to maximize this metric, tuning generation toward entity-faithful outputs without relying on human annotations. In contrast to sentence-level QA or entailment scores, EHI is lightweight, reference-free, and model-agnostic, enabling fine-grained feedback at the entity level.</p>
<p class="jove_content"></p>
<p class="jove_content">Aligning the model with an entity-level reward addresses the primary failure mode of abstractive summarization -- hallucinated entities -- and complements existing QA/NLI metrics that provide only coarse or sentence-level judgments. Experiments on meeting transcripts and news articles demonstrate consistent improvements across datasets, with a reduction in hallucinated entities and no to minimal degradation in fluency or informativeness. Because the reward does not require manual feedback, this framework provides a scalable way to increase the factual reliability of summarization systems. Entity-aware fine-tuning can thus advance the field by producing more trustworthy outputs that better support downstream QA or information-retrieval tasks.</p>
<p class="jove_content"></p>
<p class="jove_content">Few limitations must be acknowledged. The EHI metric depends on named-entity recognition; errors in entity extraction or coreference can propagate into reward calculation and may penalize models unfairly. The reward focuses exclusively on entity hallucinations and does not directly target other factual errors, such as relation or discourse inconsistencies. Reinforcement learning with narrowly defined rewards can sometimes encourage degenerate solutions; for example, fine-tuning for textual entailment alone has been reported to produce overly extractive or less informative summaries<sup class="xref">33</sup>. Although the empirical results show minimal degradations, practitioners must carefully balance faithfulness and informativeness and consider combining EHI with rewards for salience or fluency.</p>
<p class="jove_content"></p>
<p class="jove_content">Complementary approaches to hallucination mitigation could be explored alongside or instead of EHI-guided reinforcement learning. Active-learning frameworks such as Hallucination Diversity-Aware Sampling (HADAS) select diverse hallucination instances for annotation and fine-tuning, reducing the human cost of training and addressing multiple hallucination types<sup class="xref">34</sup>. Reinforcement learning with natural language inference rewards improves factual consistency but must be tuned to avoid producing extractive summaries<sup class="xref">33</sup>. Other lines of research include retrieval-augmented generation to ground responses on external documents, improved decoding strategies, and self-verification techniques. Exploring these alternatives may provide broader perspectives on hallucination mitigation and complement EHI-guided reinforcement learning.</p>
<p class="jove_content"></p>
<p class="jove_content">Improving factuality is particularly important in high-stakes domains. Hallucinated entities can mislead decision-makers in legal, medical, or scientific settings, where inaccurate summaries may propagate false information. Truthfulness-oriented research emphasizes that models must recognize uncertainty and abstain when knowledge is insufficient, especially in law or medicine<sup class="xref">35</sup>. Because EHI highlights specific entity-level errors and allows targeted corrections, fine-tuned models could be deployed in meeting transcription services, news summarization, or domain-specific summarizers to provide more faithful and trustworthy content. Moreover, the metric&#39;s model-agnostic nature makes it suitable for evaluating and improving both encoder-decoder and decoder-only architectures, encouraging widespread adoption across research and industry.</p>
<p class="jove_content"></p>
<p class="jove_content">Future work could extend this approach in several directions. Combining EHI with rewards for informativeness or relation-level factuality may balance faithfulness and content richness. Incorporating improved named-entity and coreference resolution systems will reduce reward noise. Integrating EHI with active-learning strategies, natural language inference rewards such as ternary reward schemes<sup class="xref">35</sup>&#160;-- may address broader types of hallucination and encourage abstention when appropriate. Applying EHI-guided fine-tuning to other generative tasks such as question-answering, dialogue, and cross-lingual summarization, and evaluating its effectiveness in specialized domains, such as biomedical or legal texts, will further clarify its impact and limitations.</p>
<p class="jove_content"></p>
<p class="jove_content">This study introduces a reinforcement-learning-based-based fine-tuning framework for abstractive summarization that leverages the Entity Hallucination Index (EHI) as a dense, reference-free reward signal. By comparing named entities in generated summaries against source documents and penalizing ungrounded or extraneous entities, our approach directly optimizes for entity faithfulness. Extensive experiments on multi-turn meeting transcripts and the XSUM news dataset demonstrate that EHI-based fine-tuning substantially reduces entity hallucinations, improving EHI scores by up to 61.5% on dialogue data and 136.8% on news articles-while maintaining factual consistency. Ablation studies confirm targeted reductions in negative hallucination, over-focus, and lost-focus components, alongside an increment of extractiveness. Cross-model validation across encoder-decoder and decoder-only architecture shows consistent performance gains, and correlation analyses reveal that EHI improvements align with enhanced entity recall and, for some models, improved question-answering capabilities. Qualitative examples further illustrate the elimination of fabricated entities without compromising the ability to answer content-based questions. Although the method incurs a modest recall trade-off and depends on NER accuracy, it represents a practical and scalable solution for enhancing factual reliability in neural summarization. Future work will explore integrating coreference resolution, combining EHI with informativeness rewards, and extending evaluation to specialized domains such as biomedical and legal texts.</p>                        </div>
                    </div>
                                        
